meanwhile nearly half of Americans  believe the corona virus pandemic is  affecting their mental health 19 percent  say it's having a major impact according  to a CBS poll psychologist and CBS News  contributor Lisa d'amour joins us now  from Cleveland to answer your questions  about the virus and mental health Lisa  good morning let's begin with I think  the most common mental health challenge  out there and that is Americans facing  depression viewer on Instagram asks what  advice you have for people who are right  now alone as a shelter-in-place  and suffering from long-term depression  good morning and I'm so glad that we're  answering these questions so when we  think about depression the first thing  we need to remember is that this is a  treatable mental illness but what people  may not know is that they can get  treatment right now even under stay at  home conditions typically telehealth is  something that requires a special  license something where if we're going  to work with people digitally but under  the current conditions those regulations  have been relaxed states and insurance  companies are working to make sure that  people can get mental health care so if  you or someone you love needs to work  with somebody for support reach out  through your normal referral channels  maybe your general practitioner or your  employer find yourself a good mental  health clinician and start working with  them now from home and then you can  follow up with them in person when  things return to normal the other thing  I would say right now is that we need to  be really careful about how we are  coping with this weather we have mental  health concerns  prior to this or not we want to watch  out for negative coping strategies so  any coping strategy offers relief but  negative coping strategies don't work  well over the long run so don't allow  yourself to emotionally isolate don't  count on substances to manage hard  feelings don't let yourself fall into  bad habits about not sleeping enough or  becoming sedentary instead we want to  turn to positive coping strategies  whenever we can reach out to other  people use happy distractions to manage  emotional discomfort find yourself ways  to make sure you're getting enough sleep  getting enough X  is getting outdoors when you can those  positive coping strategies are going to  make a huge difference  Lisa we've also got a question on  Facebook from Remley who regularly uses  meditation and other techniques to  manage her anxiety and but now she feels  her skills are no longer useful and she  asks what can I do to get this in check  great question so everyone's anxiety is  a little bit heightened right now and  that's a normal reaction I don't want  people to feel anxious if they're  feeling anxious that's appropriate right  now but if you feel like your normal  strategies for regulating your anxiety  are not working well what I would say to  everybody is that it's time to up your  breathing game and here's what I mean  when we are highly anxious our brain  sends a message to our heart and our  lungs to speed activity and this is  actually rooted in the ancient fighter  flight system but it's physically  uncomfortable that's what we experience  as uncomfortable anxiety it turns out  that our lungs can actually send a  message back to our brain our lungs are  covered with a network of nerves that  detect the activity of the brain of the  of the lungs of how quickly and deeply  we are breathing so if you deliberately  slow and deepen your breathing it  actually speaks to those nerves those  stretch receptors on your lungs and  sends a reassuring message back up to  the brain to sort of calm the anxiety  response so I know it can seem like  everybody talks about breathing and it  may be seem sort of small or like not a  bigot or intervention but in fact  careful and slow and deep breathing can  significantly reduce anxiety yeah at  least so that's great advice I mean  sometimes the simple things are the most  powerful things I want to sneak in one  more question here from a viewer named  Carol who asks something that I think  many of us are thinking about as we look  ahead to a time when this virus is is  gone and we can get back to something  like normal and her question is will she  ever enjoy being around strangers again  and not feel threatened this is an  interesting one because under normal  conditions the rule is that avoidance  feeds anxiety that if you have a fear  you have to face it to get past it but  right now the guidance is avoid everyone  everything and that is the right healthy  safety thing to do to really stay home  and to be very cautious about going out  when things go back to normal there may  be some people who feel frightened about  returning to life as it was before and  that will be the time for them to face  their fears the good news here is that  they don't have it all at once they can  do it in baby steps  Lisa got it baby steps a lot of us are  doing baby steps these days sue from  facebook says her family's starting to  get agitated with one another and wants  to know when will the bickering be over  what solutions can you recommend about  that for families to get along and  there's an article in USA Today where it  says yelling and spanking is up in homes  these days can you address those sure so  we're all in close quarters and people  are getting on one another's nerves so  if you can find space take a break you  should do it we have to remember that  when it comes to bickering it takes to  to bicker so just some because somebody  tosses something at you doesn't mean you  have to catch it you can refuse to  engage that way or you can say to them  start again I would say for families  with kids they may see a lot of  bickering right now and some of that may  be how kids are trying to manage boredom  kids have a lot of time on their hands  and sometimes siblings will manage their  boredom by like starting something with  one of their siblings so if you think  this might be happening I would just  call it on the carpet and I would just  say look you might be bored but  antagonizing your sister is not the  solution to that problem like go find  something else to do at least the decor  I think that's a great piece of advice  to end on and I would only add that if  you're one of those Americans still  lucky enough to have a job and you're  trying to do it from home a little  forgiveness you're not going to be as  productive as efficient as capable as  you might have been when you're going to  an office everything is disrupted so  just forgive yourself Lisa thank you  very much
 

